	Named weapons are a problem. (No, not that they make the game too
easy. You purists can go take your crysknives elsewhere, thank you very 
much.) There are too many of them, and they're too uninteresting. Out of
seventeen that currently exist, only five are worth a damn.

	These five are Fire Brand, Frost Brand, Mjollnir, Stormbringer,
and Excalibur. My strategy is always to carry one of the first four, with
Excalibur as a back-up weapon in case I run into a monster with the appro-
priate resistances. Once I've got these two, any other named weapons 
aren't worth the weight capacity they take up.

	This is an especial problem for lawful characters; they have *six*
possible weapons to get through before they get the good one -- as compared
to only two for chaotics and neutrals. (Plus it seems to me that the lawful
gods are less likely to give out weapons, although I could just be imagining
it.) Snickersnee and Orcrist are okay if you don't have access to anything
better, but we could delete Cleaver, Demonbane, Dragonbane, Giantslayer,
Grimtooth, Ogresmasher, Sunsword, Trollsbane, and Werebane from version 3.1
and it's hard to believe that anyone would ever miss them. The "slay-one-
kind-of-monster" weapons are just boring.

	The solution? What I said before: delete most of the boring weapons.
We could keep a few as "chaff" -- we don't want it to be *too* easy to get
the good weapons. Say, Grimtooth, Cleaver, Dragonbane, and Sunsword. (Sting,
of course, has to stay because of the Tolkien connection; besides, it's easy
to get rid of by naming an elven dagger.) 

	At the very least, let's make some of the "blah" weapons more inter-
esting, by giving them appropriate extra qualities. Certainly Sunsword 
(the anti-undead sword) ought to protect against level drains. Perhaps 
Werebane could protect against lycanthropy -- or even cure it. Giantslayer
could act like gauntlets of power; Dragonbane could give reflection (perhaps
reflection applying only to breath weapons).

	And if we're not going to delete the "blah" weapons, let's at least
reapportion them to be more fair to lawful characters.

